meta {
  name: Get token via api-access (public client, no secret)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{keycloakUrl}}/realms/{{realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  grant_type: password
  client_id: {{apiAccessClientId}}
  username: {{username}}
  password: {{password}}
}

script:post-response {
  // Make the token available to subsequent Payload-API data-plane calls.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200 && res.getBody() && res.getBody().access_token) {
    bru.setVar('apiAccessToken', res.getBody().access_token);
  }
}

tests {
  test("api-access issues a token without a client secret (#1293)", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("response carries an access_token", function() {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.have.property('access_token');
  });
}

docs {
  **External Payload-API access — credential contract (concept #1293).**

  Protected named-API routes (`/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}` when the dataset is NOT open data) require a
  bearer token. Per concept #1293 external API consumers must obtain that token through the static
  public Keycloak client **`api-access`** — *public client, no secret, Resource Owner Password
  Credentials (direct access grants) flow* — and NOT through the confidential `portal-frontend`
  client (whose secret is for the portal UI/management API only).

  > "the platform uses the static Keycloak client `api-access` with public client, no secret, and
  > password credentials flow." — concept #1293

  This request performs that ROPC exchange against Keycloak and stores the resulting bearer in
  `apiAccessToken`. A protected data-plane GET then sends `Authorization: Bearer {{apiAccessToken}}`.
  Note that authorization (which datasets the token's user may read) is still enforced by OPA on the
  gateway; a valid token only clears the authentication gate.
}
